WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
Kubeflow.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.929
ML pipelines as Kubernetes-native workflows.

3
00:00:04.929 --> 00:00:07.200
Powerful, and deliberately heavy.

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.433
In Kubeflow, every pipeline step is a container.

5
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:13.800
The pipeline is a graph of those steps.

6
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:20.433
Kubernetes schedules it: GPU steps land on GPU nodes, and parallel branches fan out.

7
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:23.800
Retries and artifacts are handled by the platform.

8
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.300
Around the engine sits a whole platform.

9
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.400
The pipelines engine at the base.

10
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.567
Katib for hyperparameter tuning.

11
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:33.533
KServe for model serving.

12
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.900
And notebook servers with multi-tenant namespaces.

13
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.833
Here is a pipeline in Python.

14
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:43.667
A component is a function that runs in its own container.

15
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:46.800
The pipeline function wires components together.

16
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.802
Each step becomes a pod on the cluster.

17
00:00:49.802 --> 00:00:53.267
Compile, upload, and the graph runs on Kubernetes.

18
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:56.533
Here is the contrast interviewers love.

19
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:59.046
Airflow orchestrates any tasks.

20
00:00:59.046 --> 00:01:02.400
Kubeflow is opinionated about ML on Kubernetes.

21
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:09.167
For a small team, Airflow or Prefect plus MLflow covers the loop at a tenth of the operational cost.

22
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:11.900
So when is it justified?

23
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.067
If your organization does not run Kubernetes seriously, skip it.

24
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:24.367
If it does, and many teams need shared, governed ML infrastructure with per-step GPU scheduling, Kubeflow earns its overhead.

25
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:27.567
Here is the judgment in practice.

26
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:30.761
Your solo project does not need Kubeflow.

27
00:01:30.761 --> 00:01:33.867
A GitHub Action plus MLflow covers its loop.

28
00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:41.200
But an org running forty models across six teams does, with GPU scheduling, quotas, and audited pipelines.

29
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.500
Knowing when a tool is too much is what architect means.

30
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.516
Let's recap.

31
00:01:46.516 --> 00:01:50.733
Kubeflow runs pipelines as graphs of containers on Kubernetes.

32
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:54.633
Around them sits a platform with Katib, KServe, and notebooks.

33
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:57.333
It is heavy, enterprise-scale machinery.

34
00:01:57.633 --> 00:02:00.200
And the real skill is knowing when it is overkill.
